<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RELEASE MANIFEST.

  Attributes on <application> that are security controls rather than defaults:

    android:networkSecurityConfig
        Points at res/xml/network_security_config.xml, which forbids cleartext
        and trusts only the system certificate store. The debug source set
        supplies its own copy of that resource with a loopback-only cleartext
        exception; the release variant does not contain the exception at all.

    android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        Ignored on API 24+ when a network security config is present, and
        minSdk is 24 — so this line changes no behaviour. It is here because it
        is the attribute reviewers, scanners and store review processes look
        for, and because leaving it unset would make its absence ambiguous
        rather than deliberate.

    android:allowBackup / android:fullBackupContent / android:dataExtractionRules
        Android's backup framework would copy application data off the device.
        flutter_secure_storage keeps session tokens in Keystore-backed encrypted
        preferences; moving that anywhere takes credential material out of the
        device's protected storage. Every path is therefore closed, and it takes
        all three attributes to close them, because none of them covers the
        whole surface:

          allowBackup="false" is the primary control and the one a reviewer or
          scanner looks for. It stops cloud backup and restore on every API
          level this application supports.

          fullBackupContent="false" opts out of file-based Auto Backup for
          API 23-30. Redundant behind allowBackup on those levels, and stated
          anyway so the opt-out is a decision on the page rather than a
          consequence to be worked out.

          dataExtractionRules covers what allowBackup does not. Android
          documents that for apps running on and targeting API 31 or higher,
          allowBackup="false" disables cloud backup but does not disable
          device-to-device transfer. targetSdk is 36, so that gap is live here,
          and a missing rules resource is documented to leave the transfer modes
          fully enabled rather than off. res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml
          declares every mode and excludes every domain from each; its header
          carries the reasoning and the citations.

        Nothing is lost by any of this: this client holds no local state worth
        preserving — everything authoritative is server-side.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
      WHAT KARAR DECLARES AND WHAT THE USER'S DEVICE SEES ARE NOT THE SAME SET.
      This file declares ONE permission. The merged manifest inside the
      installed artifact carries FOUR. The two must be stated separately: an
      earlier version of this comment ran them together and thereby understated
      what the installed application asks for.

      DECLARED HERE — 1:

        android.permission.INTERNET
          Protection level `normal`: granted at install, no runtime prompt, no
          user-visible consent step. The Flutter template declares it in the
          debug and profile source sets only, which would leave a release build
          with no network access whatsoever. This client exists to talk to an
          API, so the permission belongs here, in the manifest that ships.

      ADDED BY THE MANIFEST MERGER FROM DEPENDENCIES — 3. These are not
      editable from this file. Removing one means removing or replacing the
      dependency that contributes it:

        android.permission.USE_BIOMETRIC
          Protection level `normal`. Contributed by the local_auth_android
          plugin module, and independently by androidx.biometric 1.1.0 which it
          depends on. This is the permission behind the application lock.

        The deprecated pre-API-28 predecessor of USE_BIOMETRIC
          Protection level `normal`. Contributed by androidx.biometric 1.1.0
          for backwards compatibility with the API 23-27 authentication API.
          minSdk is 24, so the range it exists for is a range this application
          ships to. Its literal name must NOT be written anywhere under android/
          or ios/: the telemetry marker scan in the security suite treats that
          token as a device-tracking SDK marker throughout those trees, and the
          scan is not relaxed to accommodate a comment. The exact string belongs
          in two places instead — the merged-manifest allow-list in
          test/security/platform_hardening_test.dart, which is the assertion,
          and docs/architecture/flutter.md, which is the review record.

        com.kararfinance.app[.<environment suffix>].DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION
          Protection level `signature`, and defined BY this application rather
          than by the platform — androidx.core 1.18.0 contributes both the
          <permission> declaration and the matching <uses-permission>. It exists
          so that receivers androidx registers at runtime are reachable only by
          code signed with the same key. It grants no platform capability, is
          not a device capability the user can be asked about, and its name
          carries the applicationId suffix of whichever environment was built.

      NOTHING ELSE IS REQUESTED, by this file or by any dependency: no location,
      contacts, storage, camera, phone state, or advertising identifier. A
      permission that is never declared cannot be abused, and each of those
      would create a privacy disclosure obligation the product has no need to
      take on.

      THIS COMMENT IS NOT THE CONTROL. Prose drifts from artifacts; that is how
      the earlier claim survived. The control is the merged-manifest assertion
      in test/security/platform_hardening_test.dart, which reads a real build
      output and matches the permission set exactly, so a dependency that starts
      contributing a fourth platform permission fails the build instead of
      reaching a device unreviewed.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <application
        android:label="karar_mobile"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
